  "note": "Read from the darktable 5.6 user manual (the current released manual). darktable has no single \"default shortcuts\" table: the shortcuts page (preferences & settings > shortcuts) documents how the shortcut system works and only names a handful of predefined bindings, so the default bindings here were collected by walking the whole manual - every page listed in https://docs.darktable.org/usermanual/5.6/en/sitemap.xml was fetched and searched for documented key bindings, and the pages that actually carry one are the pages listed in sources. Note on version aliases: the /usermanual/stable/ alias currently redirects to the 4.8 build, whose pages all return HTTP 404 from docs.darktable.org, and /usermanual/5.0, /5.2 and /5.4 redirect to the development build; the 5.6 tree is the newest one that serves real pages, so 5.6 URLs are cited. Context is the darktable view or module the manual attaches the shortcut to (lighttable, lighttable filemanager, zoomable lighttable, lighttable culling, darkroom, map, slideshow, filmstrip, tagging, metadata editor, module controls, parametric masks, locations, find location, import, styles, timeline, log history, preferences, preferences > presets, shortcut mapping screen); \"\" is used for the shortcuts the manual documents as working in any darktable view (view switching, panel visibility, fullscreen, the H help overlay, the MIDI input-system reset). The lighttable and filmstrip lists are deliberately near-duplicates because the manual documents them separately for each context and they differ: only the filmstrip list carries Ctrl+A / Ctrl+Shift+A / Ctrl+I selection and Ctrl+X \"copies the settings of the last changed module\", and only the lighttable list carries D, W and Ctrl+W. Rows that document a set of keys become one action per key with the key repeated in parentheses - the star ratings 0-5, the two ways to leave sticky preview mode (F and ESC), the slideshow delay keys (+ / up arrow and - / down arrow), the arrow keys that adjust a slider, and the two keys that detach a tag (Delete and BackSpace). macOS values are given only for chords containing Ctrl, because the shortcuts page states as a blanket rule that on macOS shortcuts use cmd instead of ctrl and option instead of alt; no per-shortcut macOS variant is documented anywhere in the manual, so chords without Ctrl carry no mac value. Excluded, and why: everything mouse-driven (click, Ctrl+click, Shift+click, Ctrl+Shift+click, right-click, double-click, click+drag, scroll, Ctrl+scroll, Shift+scroll, Shift+Ctrl+scroll, Shift+Alt+scroll, Ctrl+Alt+wheel, middle-click) - the bulk of what the processing- and utility-module pages document is of this kind; hold-a-key-while-dragging bindings (the \"a\" key that forces pan & zoom in the drawn masks, retouch and tone equalizer modules, Ctrl+Shift held while dragging a module to a new pixelpipe position, Ctrl held while zooming to lift the 100% limit, and Shift or Ctrl held while adjusting to scale a slider's step size by 10x or 0.1x), because the chord grammar cannot express a modifier held across a drag; the brush-mask keys { } < > and the culling mode-switch key <, which are not in the chord grammar's punctuation set; the slideshow \"right shift-key\" and \"left shift-key\" bindings, which the grammar cannot tell apart; and the touchpad pinch and two-finger gestures. Two documented statements conflict over Ctrl+B in the darkroom: the color assessment page and the workflow > process page both give it as the color assessment toggle, while the shortcuts page uses \"ctrl + b\" in an illustrative example of the action views/darkroom/guide lines/toggle. The corroborated color assessment binding is kept and the guide lines toggle is left out as ambiguous. Nothing was carried over from an older manual, and no binding was inferred from darktable's source code or from the in-app shortcut list.",
